    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Brita rates its standard pitcher filter for 40 gallons, which it equates to about two months, and its Elite filter, formerly sold as Longlast+, for 120 gallons, or about six months. Stream filters follow the standard 40-gallon, two-month schedule. Those month figures assume average use, so a busy household or hard water can use a filter up well before the calendar says, while a light user can safely go by gallons. Here is how to work out your real schedule, the signs a filter is spent, and what each one costs per gallon.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: 40 Gallons for Standard, 120 for Elite
          </h2>

          <p>
            The schedule depends on which filter is in the pitcher, and the box tells you. Brita's standard white filter is rated for 40 gallons, roughly two months for an average household. The Elite filter, the grey one that was called Longlast+ until Brita renamed it, is rated for 120 gallons, roughly six months. The Stream filters used in Brita's filter-as-you-pour pitchers are rated like the standard filter: 40 gallons, about two months.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Brita filter</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Rated capacity</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Brita's time guide</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best for</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Standard</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40 gallons</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 2 months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Chlorine taste and odour, low cost per filter</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Elite (formerly Longlast+)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120 gallons</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 6 months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lead and a longer contaminant list, fewer changes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Stream</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40 gallons</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 2 months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Filtering as you pour, no waiting</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Faucet-mount</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100 gallons</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 4 months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher-volume use at the sink</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The gallon figure is the real limit; the months are a convenience. Filters are tested and certified up to their rated capacity, so the claims on the box apply to the first 40 or 120 gallons, not to however many weeks you happen to keep the cartridge in. If you drink a lot of water, you can reach 40 gallons in a month. If you live alone and mostly drink coffee, the same filter can last longer than two months.
          </p>

          <p>
            The two main filters are not interchangeable in what they do, only in the slot they fit. According to Brita's own performance claims, the standard filter targets chlorine taste and odour plus a few metals such as copper, cadmium and mercury, but it is not the one to buy if you are concerned about lead. The Elite is certified for lead reduction and a longer list of contaminants. Check the performance data sheet for the exact list on the version you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the short answer is to change a standard or Stream filter every 40 gallons or two months, whichever comes first, and an Elite every 120 gallons or six months, whichever comes first. If you are comparing pitcher brands on that basis, our <a href="/best-water-filter-pitcher/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best water filter pitchers</a> guide puts filter capacity next to cost and certification.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="gallons-or-months" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Should You Go by Gallons or by Months?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Go by whichever arrives first, and work out which that is for your household once. A gallon is 16 cups, so 40 gallons is 640 cups. In a 10-cup pitcher that is about 64 fills; in a 6-cup pitcher, about 106. The Elite's 120 gallons is roughly 192 fills of a 10-cup pitcher. Count roughly how many times a day you refill and you have your real schedule.
          </p>

          <p>
            Brita's two-month figure works out to about two-thirds of a gallon a day, which is roughly one fill of a 10-cup pitcher daily. A household that refills it twice a day will use a standard filter up in about a month, and an Elite in about three months. A household that fills it every other day can stretch a standard filter past the two-month mark on gallons alone, though not indefinitely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not stretch a filter too far on low use. A cartridge that sits wet in the fridge for many months is not the same as a fresh one even if it has filtered few gallons, because the carbon keeps picking up whatever is in the water and the damp cartridge is a place microbes can grow. Treat the time figure as an outer limit, not a suggestion you can ignore because the pitcher sees light use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Count everything that goes through the pitcher, not just drinking water. If the pitcher fills the kettle, the coffee maker, the pet bowl and the cooking pot, those gallons count. Many people who say their filter does not last two months are filtering far more than they drink. If that is you, a faucet-mount filter or a larger dispenser may suit you better than a pitcher.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hard-water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Does Hard Water Mean Changing It Sooner?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Often, yes. Hard water carries more dissolved calcium and magnesium, and very hard or mineral-heavy water can load up a filter's media faster and clog it sooner. Hard water and water with a lot of sediment are the usual reasons a filter slows down or stops performing before it reaches its rated gallons. If your kettle scales up quickly, assume your filter will run short of the full rating.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is worth knowing what a pitcher filter does and does not do about hardness. It is not a water softener. A pitcher may reduce some hardness for a while, which is why kettle scale often eases at first, but its small cartridge cannot keep up with genuinely hard water for long. If scale is your main problem around the whole house, the answer is a softener; see <a href="/how-long-do-water-softeners-last/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long water softeners last</a> for what owning one involves.
          </p>

          <p>
            In a hard-water home, watch the kettle as much as the calendar. If you use filtered water in it and white scale starts reappearing faster than it did with a fresh cartridge, that is a reasonable sign the filter is spent. Our <a href="/best-electric-kettle/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best electric kettles</a> guide notes which models are easiest to descale when that happens.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sediment works the same way. Older pipes, a recent main repair, or water that is occasionally cloudy will push particles into the cartridge and slow the flow. If that happens shortly after a change, it is not necessarily a faulty filter. A plumbing problem or a supply issue is worth checking with your utility if the cloudiness keeps coming back.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Can You Tell a Brita Filter Needs Changing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The clearest sign is taste. The standard filter's main job is removing chlorine taste and odour, so when filtered water starts tasting like the tap again, the carbon is spent, whatever the indicator says. Pour a glass of tap water and a glass of filtered water side by side and compare them; if you cannot tell them apart, change the filter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flow rate is the next clue. A filter that takes noticeably longer than it used to is usually clogged with sediment or minerals. A standard filter that suddenly lets water rush straight through is a different problem: it may not be seated properly, or water may be channelling around the media, and either way it is not filtering well. Reseat it first, and replace it if the speed does not return to normal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Elite filters are slower by design, and a very slow Elite is sometimes trapped air rather than a spent cartridge. Removing it, holding it under running water, and reseating it often fixes that. If the flow stays sluggish after the filter is properly primed and seated, and it is near the end of its gallons or months, it is time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some things look alarming but are not signs of a failed filter. Tiny black specks, especially in the first few fills, are usually loose particles of activated carbon and are generally considered harmless; rinse or prime the next filter more thoroughly. Green slime or film in the reservoir is algae or biofilm in the pitcher itself, which means it needs a proper wash and a spot out of sunlight, not just a new cartridge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, the indicator. Brita's filter indicators are reminders, not sensors that test your water: they estimate based on time or use, depending on the model. Reset or set it whenever you change the filter, and if your electronic indicator has a setting for the filter type, make sure it matches, or an Elite will be flagged at two months instead of six.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-happens" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Happens If You Do Not Change It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nothing dramatic happens on day 61. The filter does not stop working all at once; it gradually does less. The certified reduction claims only apply up to the rated capacity, so past 40 or 120 gallons there is no assurance the filter is still reducing what the box says it does. For chlorine taste, you will notice. For lead or other metals, you will not, which is the real reason to respect the rating.
          </p>

          <p>
            A spent filter also becomes a better home for bacteria. Activated carbon removes chlorine, the disinfectant that keeps tap water's bacteria count down, and the damp cartridge holds organic matter that microbes can feed on. Studies of carbon pitcher filters have found bacterial levels in filtered water can be higher than in the tap water itself. For healthy people on treated city water that is not usually considered a health risk, but it is a good reason not to run a cartridge for months past its limit.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is also why storage matters. Keep the pitcher in the fridge rather than on the counter, especially in summer, and do not leave filtered water standing for days. Warm, still water without chlorine is exactly what bacteria prefer, and a filter that has been in use for a long time gives them more to work with.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical risk depends on why you filter. If it is just for taste, an overdue filter mostly means water that tastes like the tap again. If you bought an Elite specifically for lead, an overdue filter means you may be drinking water you meant to treat, with no way to tell by taste. In that case, follow the gallon count strictly and mark the change date on the pitcher.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Much Does a Brita Filter Cost Per Gallon?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Filter prices vary by retailer and pack size, so do the maths on what you actually pay. Divide the price of one filter by its rated gallons. Standard filters bought in multipacks commonly work out to somewhere around 12 to 20 cents per gallon; Elite filters, which cost more each but last three times as long, usually land in a similar range or slightly lower. Single filters bought one at a time cost more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both are far cheaper than bottled water, and both are cheaper per gallon if you change them on schedule than if you pay for a filter that ends up working poorly. The cost difference between the two filter types is small enough that the choice should rest on what you want removed and how often you want to change it, not on price alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Here is the simple way to decide. If you want better-tasting water and do not mind changing a filter every couple of months, the standard filter is fine. If you are concerned about lead, especially in an older house with lead service lines or old solder, or you would simply rather change the filter twice a year, the Elite is the better buy. If your water has a sulfur smell, neither is a real fix; see <a href="/why-does-my-water-smell-like-rotten-eggs/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">why your water smells like rotten eggs</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you find yourself filtering several gallons a day, a pitcher becomes the expensive and inconvenient option. An under-sink system or whole-home filter costs more up front but much less per gallon at high volume and does not need refilling. Our <a href="/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best water filtration systems for home</a> guide covers those.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tips" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Do You Get the Most Out of Each Filter?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Prepare each new filter the way the package says, because standard and Elite filters have different instructions. Priming and rinsing remove loose carbon and air, which helps the filter reach its full flow and performance. Many people also discard the first fill. Seat the filter firmly; a filter that is not pushed fully home lets water bypass it, which defeats the point.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use cold tap water only. Brita filters are designed for cold water, and hot water can damage the media and release what the filter has captured. Also note that Brita filters are intended for water that is already microbiologically safe, such as treated city water. They are not a way to make untested well water or water under a boil notice safe to drink.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wash the pitcher every time you change the filter, or more often if you see film. Use warm soapy water on the reservoir, lid and filter housing, rinse well, and dry it before refitting. Hand-wash any lid with an electronic indicator. Do not wash the filter cartridge with soap, and keep the pitcher out of direct sunlight, which encourages algae.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep a record. Writing the change date on a strip of tape on the pitcher, or putting a recurring reminder on your phone, is more reliable than trusting memory or a sticker indicator. Buy filters in multipacks so a spare is always on hand; the most common reason people run a filter for six months is that they did not have a replacement when it was due.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are on a well, test your water before deciding what to filter it with, and treat the house rather than the glass if the test shows problems. If you are on city water and only want better taste, a pitcher changed on schedule does the job well. Our <a href="/best-water-filter-pitcher/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">water filter pitcher rankings</a> compare Brita with the alternatives.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Going by months when you use a lot</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The two-month guide assumes about one pitcher fill a day. A household that refills twice a day reaches 40 gallons in about a month, and its filter will be past its rating long before the indicator flashes. Count your fills once, divide 640 cups by your pitcher's size, and set your schedule by the result.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the indicator over your taste</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Filter indicators estimate based on time or use; they do not test the water. If filtered water tastes like chlorine again, or the indicator was never reset after the last change, believe your taste. Conversely, set the indicator to the right filter type, or an Elite will be flagged as spent four months early.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the standard filter for lead</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The standard Brita filter is aimed at chlorine taste and odour and a few metals, and it is not the one certified for lead. If lead is your concern, use the Elite or another filter certified to NSF/ANSI 53 for lead, check the performance data sheet, and change it strictly by the gallon count.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filtering hot water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Running hot water through a pitcher filter to speed up cooking or tea can damage the media and reduce what the filter removes. Brita filters are designed for cold water. Filter cold, then heat it. The same goes for leaving the pitcher on a warm counter, which encourages bacteria in water that no longer has chlorine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Changing the filter but not washing the pitcher</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A fresh cartridge in a pitcher with slime in the reservoir starts life already contaminated. Every time you change the filter, wash the reservoir, lid and housing in warm soapy water, rinse and dry them, and hand-wash any lid with electronics. Keep the pitcher in the fridge and away from sunlight.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a Brita Elite filter last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Brita rates the Elite filter, formerly called Longlast+, for 120 gallons, which it equates to about six months for an average household. A household that refills a 10-cup pitcher twice a day could reach 120 gallons in about three months. Change it at 120 gallons or six months, whichever comes first, and make sure any electronic indicator is set for an Elite filter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a Brita filter longer than two months?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If your household uses well under a pitcher a day, you can reasonably go by gallons and run a standard filter somewhat past two months until it reaches 40 gallons. Do not stretch it indefinitely, because a damp carbon cartridge left for many months can harbour bacteria. If taste changes or flow slows before then, replace it regardless of the calendar.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it bad to drink from an expired Brita filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people on treated city water it is not dangerous, but you lose the benefit you bought it for. Past its rated gallons, the filter's certified reduction claims no longer apply, chlorine taste returns, and bacteria can build up in the cartridge. If you use an Elite specifically for lead, an overdue filter is a real concern because you cannot taste lead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my Brita filter so slow?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A slow standard filter is usually clogged with sediment or minerals, often near the end of its life or earlier in hard water. A slow Elite filter is frequently trapped air: remove it, hold it under running water, and reseat it firmly. If flow stays poor after priming and reseating, especially near the end of the rated gallons, replace the filter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are the black specks in my Brita water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Small black specks are usually loose particles of activated carbon from the filter, most common in the first few fills of a new cartridge. They are generally considered harmless. Priming and rinsing the filter as the package directs and discarding the first fill reduces them. If specks keep appearing well into a filter's life, try reseating it or replacing it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a Brita filter remove hard water minerals?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in any lasting way. A pitcher filter may reduce some hardness for a while, which can ease kettle scale at first, but it is not a water softener and its small cartridge cannot keep up with genuinely hard water. Hard water can also use up a filter sooner than its rating. For whole-house scale problems, a water softener is the proper fix.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do unopened Brita filters expire?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Kept sealed in their original pouch, spare filters keep well, which is why multipacks are practical. The clock starts when the filter gets wet. Store spares somewhere cool and dry, out of direct sunlight, and keep them sealed until you need them. If a pouch has been damaged or opened, prime and rinse that filter thoroughly before use.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

